From 16bdce568a960a18d8216957e89daa360844686f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vauzi-17 <189964753+Vauzi-17@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 22:34:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] ntsync: add a userspace backend (libntsync_android) for
 devices without /dev/ntsync

Android kernels generally don't ship the ntsync driver, so the in-process
synchronization backported from wine-cachyos would do nothing there. This adds
GameNative's userspace ntsync library as a second backend for it, chosen by
wineserver once at startup:

  1. PROTON_NO_NTSYNC=1 (or WINE_DISABLE_FAST_SYNC=1 / WINENTSYNC=0) disables
     ntsync; esync/fsync work again if WINEESYNC/WINEFSYNC ask for them.
  2. /dev/ntsync is used when it opens *and* a probe NTSYNC_IOC_CREATE_EVENT
     succeeds (the node can exist but be blocked by SELinux or seccomp).
     PROTON_NO_KERNEL_NTSYNC=1 skips it, for A/B testing.
  3. Otherwise ntsync_init() attaches to the shared region
     ($NTSYNC_SHM, else $TMPDIR/ntsync_userspace.vN.shm) and every in-process
     sync object becomes a u32 handle into it.
  4. If that fails too, wineserver falls back to server-side synchronization.

The library mirrors the kernel ioctl interface with handles in place of fds,
so the rest of the code is unchanged: objects still carry an int that is an
fd for the kernel and a handle for userspace, and ntsync_userspace_ioctl()
maps each ioctl request onto the matching library call. wineserver creates
and destroys every object; clients learn the backend and the object handles
from two new reply fields (get_linux_sync_device.userspace and
get_linux_sync_obj.userspace_handle) and never close a handle themselves.
They still hold a server handle to each object they cache, so lifetimes work
exactly as with the kernel.

The client resolves the backend before its first object lookup, since
NtSetEvent and friends can run before any wait has asked for the device, and
reads the cached device with acquire semantics so the backend flag written
before it is visible on arm64.

configure detects the library (HAVE_NTSYNC_ANDROID, NTSYNC_ANDROID_LIBS), so
non-Android builds are unaffected. build-step-arm64ec.sh gains
--build-ntsync-android and fails the build if configure did not detect the
library or if the installed wineserver/ntdll.so lack the userspace code.
